"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,418\nPaper or screen?\n\n4\n00:00:12,218 --> 00:00:15,171\nMore and more course reading happens on screens.\n\n5\n00:00:15,486 --> 00:00:18,603\nTextbooks, articles, slides, all online.\n\n6\n00:00:18,949 --> 00:00:20,761\nDoes it matter where students read?\n\n7\n00:00:20,931 --> 00:00:23,965\nIn 2018, Pablo Delgado,\n\n8\n00:00:24,283 --> 00:00:34,060\nCristina Vargas, Rakefet Ackerman and Ladislao Salmeron pooled fifty four studies with one hundred seventy one thousand and fifty five participants,\n\n9\n00:00:34,403 --> 00:00:38,098\ncomparing the same texts on paper and on screens.\n\n10\n00:00:38,268 --> 00:00:40,402\nReaders understood more on paper.\n\n11\n00:00:40,816 --> 00:00:42,191\nThe effect was small,\n\n12\n00:00:42,407 --> 00:00:44,934\nan effect size of about zero point two,\n\n13\n00:00:44,954 --> 00:00:46,525\nbut it was consistent.\n\n14\n00:00:47,156 --> 00:00:54,065\nA separate meta-analysis by Virginia Clinton in 2019 found a similar paper advantage.\n\n15\n00:00:54,235 --> 00:00:55,497\nThe details matter.\n\n16\n00:00:55,907 --> 00:00:59,235\nThe paper advantage showed up for informational texts,\n\n17\n00:00:59,441 --> 00:01:01,513\nthe kind assigned in most courses,\n\n18\n00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:04,200\nbut not for narrative texts like stories.\n\n19\n00:01:04,537 --> 00:01:06,241\nIt grew under time pressure.\n\n20\n00:01:06,584 --> 00:01:08,131\nAnd across the years studied,\n\n21\n00:01:08,151 --> 00:01:09,296\nit did not shrink.\n\n22\n00:01:09,614 --> 00:01:11,124\nIf anything, it grew.\n\n23\n00:01:11,294 --> 00:01:11,854\nWhy?\n\n24\n00:01:11,975 --> 00:01:15,876\nOne idea is shallowing: we use screens mostly for quick,\n\n25\n00:01:15,913 --> 00:01:18,802\nskimming reading, and carry that habit into study.\n\n26\n00:01:19,359 --> 00:01:23,357\nAnother is that screen readers feel they understood more than they did,\n\n27\n00:01:23,532 --> 00:01:25,140\nso they stop too soon.\n\n28\n00:01:25,310 --> 00:01:26,281\nSome caution.\n\n29\n00:01:26,646 --> 00:01:27,759\nThe effect is small,\n\n30\n00:01:27,779 --> 00:01:29,140\nand it is an average.\n\n31\n00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:31,452\nGood screen habits can narrow it,\n\n32\n00:01:31,472 --> 00:01:34,103\nand digital text has real advantages: cost,\n\n33\n00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:38,253\nsearch, and accessibility tools for many students.\n\n34\n00:01:38,423 --> 00:01:39,209\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:39,526 --> 00:01:42,019\nFor key readings, offer a printable version,\n\n36\n00:01:42,039 --> 00:01:43,999\nor encourage students to print.\n\n37\n00:01:44,462 --> 00:01:47,183\nAvoid tight time limits on screen reading.\n\n38\n00:01:47,594 --> 00:01:52,310\nAsk students to slow down and check their understanding with a question or summary.\n\n39\n00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:57,683\nOne tip to share with students: print or slow down for hard reading.\n\n40\n00:01:57,853 --> 00:01:59,629\nSkim on screens if you like.\n\n41\n00:01:59,969 --> 00:02:02,096\nStudy at the speed of understanding.\n\n"